# Creating Diagrams with TikZ, pgfplots, and tikz-cd

Vector figures produced by TikZ match the document's fonts and scale without pixelation — the 2026 standard for publication-quality diagrams. This skill covers the three packages that cover ~95% of academic figures: **TikZ** (block diagrams, flowcharts, geometry), **pgfplots** (function and data plots), and **tikz-cd** (commutative diagrams).

## 1. Preamble Setup

TikZ is modular: load the package, then load only the libraries you use. Check the document preamble first (`read_file` / `get_tex_structure`) and add only what is missing via `make_edits`.

```latex
\usepackage{tikz}
\usetikzlibrary{positioning, arrows.meta, calc, fit, shapes.geometric}
```

For data plots:
```latex
\usepackage{pgfplots}
\pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}   % silence "you should set compat" warnings
```

For commutative diagrams:
```latex
\usepackage{tikz-cd}
```

**Do not assume `pgfplots` is pulled in transitively by `tikz` — it is not.** Each of the three packages must be explicitly loaded.

## 2. Reusing Styles

Define styles once in a `\tikzset` block (document-wide) or in the optional argument of a single `tikzpicture`. Never repeat raw options on every node.

```latex
\tikzset{
  block/.style={draw, rectangle, rounded corners, minimum height=1cm,
                minimum width=2cm, align=center, fill=blue!8},
  arr/.style={-Stealth, thick},
}
```

## 3. Block / Architecture Diagram

```latex
\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=1.2cm and 1.6cm]
  \node[block] (input) {Input $x$};
  \node[block, right=of input] (model) {Encoder};
  \node[block, right=of model] (output) {Output $\hat{y}$};
  \draw[arr] (input) -- (model);
  \draw[arr] (model) -- (output);
\end{tikzpicture}
```

Keys: `right=of input` needs the `positioning` library; `-Stealth` needs `arrows.meta`. Naming nodes `(input)` makes later edges readable.

## 4. Data and Function Plots (pgfplots)

```latex
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \begin{axis}[
    xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$f(x)$},
    domain=-3:3, samples=80,
    grid=both, grid style={gray!20},
    legend pos=north west, width=0.8\linewidth,
  ]
    \addplot[blue, thick] {x^2};     \addlegendentry{$x^2$}
    \addplot[red, dashed] {x^3};     \addlegendentry{$x^3$}
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
```

For tabular data, replace `\addplot {...}` with `\addplot table[x=col1, y=col2] {data.dat};` or inline coordinates `\addplot coordinates {(0,0) (1,1) (2,4)};`. Prefer `pgfplots` over importing raster screenshots of Matplotlib output so the figure stays vector and its fonts match the text.

## 5. Commutative Diagrams (tikz-cd)

```latex
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large, row sep=large]
  A \arrow[r, "f"] \arrow[d, "\alpha"'] & B \arrow[d, "\beta"] \\
  C \arrow[r, "g"']                       & D
\end{tikzcd}
```

The prime in `"\alpha"'` flips a label to the other side of the arrow. `tikz-cd` is purpose-built for this — never hand-roll commutative arrows in raw TikZ.

## 6. Flowcharts and Decision Nodes

```latex
\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=0.9cm]
  \node[draw, ellipse, fill=green!15] (start) {Start};
  \node[draw, rectangle, below=of start, align=center] (p) {Train model};
  \node[draw, diamond, aspect=2, below=of p, fill=yellow!15] (d) {Acc.\,$>0.9$?};
  \node[draw, ellipse, below=of d, fill=green!15] (stop) {Stop};
  \draw[arr] (start) -- (p);
  \draw[arr] (p) -- (d);
  \draw[arr] (d) -- node[right]{Yes} (stop);
  \draw[arr] (d.east) -- ++(1.6,0) |- node[pos=0.25, above]{No} (p.east);
\end{tikzpicture}
```

`diamond` and `ellipse` need `shapes.geometric`. The `|-` connector routes a path right-then-down.

## 7. Loops and Repeated Elements

Use `\foreach` instead of copy-pasting nodes:

```latex
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \foreach \i [count=\n] in {a,b,c,d}
    \node[draw, circle] at (\n, 0) {$\i$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

## 8. Placing the Figure

Wrap the `tikzpicture` in a `figure` float. If a figure is too wide, scale it with `\resizebox` rather than re-deriving all coordinates.

```latex
\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{%
    \begin{tikzpicture}
      % ...diagram...
    \end{tikzpicture}%
  }
  \caption{System overview. Solid arrows denote data flow.}
  \label{fig:overview}
\end{figure}
```

## 9. TeXposit-Specific Constraints and Pitfalls

**The `external` library is forbidden here.** `\usetikzlibrary{external}` + `\tikzexternalize` requires `--shell-escape`, and TeXposit always compiles with `-no-shell-escape` for security. It will fail. For large/slow figures, do **not** reach for externalization — instead move the figure into its own small `.tex` file (via `create_file`) and `\input` it, so only edited figures recompile.

Other frequent compile failures:

- **Missing semicolons.** Every `\draw`, `\path`, `\node`, and `\fill` must end with `;`. A missing `;` cascades errors into the next line.
- **Library not loaded.** Using `-Stealth` without `arrows.meta`, `right=of` without `positioning`, or `diamond` without `shapes.geometric` each produce errors. Add the library to the preamble, not the body.
- **Multi-line node text without `align`.** `\\` inside a node does nothing unless the node has `align=center` (or `left`/`right`).
- **pgfplots compat warnings.** Set `\pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}` once — older defaults change behaviour and emit warnings every compile.
- **Overfull hbox from oversized figures.** Wrap in `\resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{...}` or set `width=0.8\linewidth` on the axis.
- **Math in nodes.** Node text is text mode by default; `$...$` inside `{...}` is fine. Forgetting `$` around symbols like `\alpha` yields "Undefined control sequence".

## 10. Workflow with TeXposit Tools

1. `get_tex_structure` (or `read_file` on the main `.tex`) — find the preamble; confirm `tikz`/`pgfplots`/`tikz-cd` and the needed `\usetikzlibrary{...}` are present.
2. `make_edits` — add missing packages/libraries to the preamble, then insert the `figure` block at the target location.
3. `verify_latex` on the touched file — confirm zero syntax errors before relying on a compile. TikZ figures compile on every run (no caching without `external`), so keep figures simple enough to build in a few seconds.
4. After the user compiles, check the result in the LAST COMPILATION STATUS section or the `.log` under `output/` (`read_file`). If it reports a missing library or macro, fix the preamble rather than editing the figure in place blindly.

When unsure which library a style needs, prefer the minimal preamble above and add libraries one at a time as `verify_latex` reports them — this keeps the diff small and the cause of each error obvious.

